A||N||12||Mattsfiero||HELP!!! Coolant problem||||4||
Z||000000||Mattsfiero||01-29-2001||08:13 AM||||Hi Everyone,<BR>  Well I have a slight problem that I fear could become worst if it is not addressed. My temp gauge started going nuts last night going from cold to almost redline and then to normal temp. My guess is that somehow I have managed to get an aitbubble into my coolant system and now I am going to nee to bleed the entire thing out to remove it. Well here is the major thing. I have never bled anything like the fiero with it's remote radiator. Can anyone tell me the proper way to bleed or flush the system to rid it of the air bubbles.<P>Thanks in advance<P>------------------<BR>Matt<BR>85 custom coupe<BR>Mattsfiero@excite.com<BR>www.anglefire.com/ma3/mattsfiero||||reg||4
Z||000001||HAGS||01-29-2001||08:21 AM||||Sounds like you know what your talking about so you probably know this... when the engine reaches a certain temperature it triggers your coolant fan to turn on and it will cool it down then shutoff, then the engine will heat back up again...when I was brand new to cars it scared me going up fast and then going down...of course I had some sort of leaking problem I think because at stops or lights the coolant couldnt handle it by itself, it needed to be moving.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mattsfiero||01-29-2001||08:27 AM||||I did think of the fan however the Temp change is too Quick and the fan is constant and not shutting down. the temp floats around 180-190 when driving normal (And at 155,000 it has had its uses) I am almost certain it is the coolant. Thank you for the fast reply.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Matt<BR>85 custom coupe<BR>Mattsfiero@aol.com<BR>www.anglefire.com/ma3/mattsfiero||||reg||10
Z||000003||TheFiero64||01-29-2001||11:11 AM||||If you think that there is air in the system. Wait until the car cools down and open the thermostat housing in the back and add coolant if necessary. If you park on a slight incline with the back higher than the front it will help.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||4
Z||000004||TheFiero64||01-29-2001||11:12 AM||||Also check the coolant level in the catch can up front.||||reg||4
Z||000005||sqoach||01-29-2001||11:40 AM||||orge's cave has everything you need to know about this subject. just click on the link at the bottom of this page.<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig2.JPG"> ||||reg||10
Z||000006||tgowens||01-29-2001||11:41 AM||||if there is no coolant in the overflow tank in the front you may be low on coolant. You can add coolant to this tank and it will be returned to the system by vacuum. If you suspect that you are low on coolant, remove the cap on the engine thermostat housing and add coolant, cap it back, run the car for about 30 to 45 seconds, remove the cap and check the level again. Add coolant if necessary and repeat the process three or four times, this is what we call "burping" the system..........this will remove the air from the system. when the coolant level remains constantly filled you have done a good job!<P>Also, you didn't say if the "Service Engine Soon" light came on or not - if so, you could have a bad coolant temperature sensor......<P>It is also possible that the temperature sending unit is malfunctioning - check the two wires just to the left of and slightly below the #5 plug on the engine - the plug tends to become brittle and break due to the heat from the manifold........<P>Just some things to look for.........hope this helps.........<P>Also, you do know that on a Fiero when you begin to heat up (as in stop and go traffic) you can turn the air conditioner on and it will start the cooling fan to reduce engine temperature...........||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||01-29-2001||07:37 PM||||If you appear to be losing coolant and can't find an obvious leak check the heater core.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Carrolles||01-30-2001||06:42 PM||||Have you put a new radiator cap on recently?  If so you probably got the vented one that Stant lists for Fieros.  Our cars use a non-vented cap at the radiator.<P>A vented cap allows coolant to siphon into the overflow reservoir and overflow it onto the ground thus losing coolant.  When the engine cools it sucks air backinto the system.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Mattsfiero||02-03-2001||11:50 AM||||Once again I thank everyone greatly for there advise. I checked the overflow tank up front and it was very low I added some anti-freeze and the problem seems to have ended. I will be flushing it as soon as the weather gets a little warmer around here just to be sure. One member asked if the Engine light came on and the answer is yes it does only at Idle the code it is giving me is my Idle Air Control or IAC valve wich is brand new and seems to be operating correctly ...(The idle is at about 950-1000rpm) once agin THANK you everyone<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Matt<BR>85 custom coupe<BR>Mattsfiero@aol.com<BR>www.anglefire.com/ma3/mattsfiero||||reg||10
Z||000010||theogre||02-03-2001||12:06 PM||||An IAC error with a known good IAC is most likely a vacuum leak. could be wiring or ecm. You can get a "noid" light to test the ECM wiring. It's about $10. Pep Boys actually has them with their tool disply here. You might have to order it. It's made by the same company most Noid lights for the EFI are made by. The company is called OTC.||||reg||1
Z||000011||sqoach||02-03-2001||08:30 PM||||remember when you change something, the computer has to be reset, or it will still show the code. just disconnect the negative for about 10 seconds, then reconnect. of course if you've done this, then i don't know.<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Mattsfiero||02-05-2001||07:46 AM||||Well when I installed the new IAC I removed the negative from the battery before replacing it and reconnected afterward. That should have reset it if not when I unplugged the battery to change my Starter Celluniod that should have but It is probably just a vacum leak though.<P>Thanks again<P>------------------<BR>Matt<BR>85 custom coupe<BR>Mattsfiero@aol.com<BR>www.anglefire.com/ma3/mattsfiero||||reg||10
